 First, notice that if we have an arithmetic sequence with a common difference of 6, any two consecutive terms will be congruent to either 0 or 6 modulo  6. So we cannot have an arithmetic sequence spanning all of the prime numbers (because otherwise one of each pair of neighbors, i. e. $(n, n + 1)$ or $(n, n + 5)$, would have to be two distinct primes).
For a few cases, we find one prime for each starting value from $1$ through $35$.  And the last one is $35 \cdot 6 + 23 = 233$, so we need only keep any that start from $1$ through $34$.  These are:
$1, 7, 13, 19, 25$ ($25$ not prime);
$3, 9,    15, 21, 27$ ($9, 15, 21, 27$ not prime);
$5, 11, 17, 23, 29$;
$7, 13, 19, 25, 31$ ($25$ not prime);
$9, 15, 21, 27, 33$ ($9, 15, 21, 27, 33$ not prime);
$11, 17, 23, 29, 35$ ($35$ not prime);
You should be able to find a pattern here. The first set of numbers can be set up as:
$$3k+1, \ \ 3k + 7, \ \ 3k + 13, \ \ 3k + 19, \ \ 3k + 25$$
You should go and prove why this is only a sequence that works if $k \equiv 1 \pmod{5}$. To come to a coverter for reasonig, the expressions $k \equiv 1, 6, 11, 16, 21 \pmod{25}$ all work, since $21 \equiv 21 \pmod{25}$ and the others are congruent mod $5$.  
You should also prove why having just two terms in the same sequence guarantees that they cannot be prime (i. e. $(n, n + 6)$ or $(n, n + 4)$ could not be consecutive prime numbers).  

If the common difference is 1 and not prime, we know one of the numbers will be even, so $2$ will be one of the terms.  So one example is just $2, 3, 5, 7, 11$.  Similarly, if both differenced are %4 un-prime, the second will not be prime, so an example is $3, 7, 11, 15, 19$.
If the difference is 2 and not prime, the second term would be even, so $2$ would be a factor, and not be prime, so no sequences will work.  If both is %4 un-prime, again one of the terms will be even, so we are finished.

Note: We really only need one example.  If the common difference is $k < 21$, the first example is just
$$k, 2k + 2, 3k + 2, 4k + 2, 5k + 2.$$

Note, Actually only one example, since for $k=7$ and $k=13$, we are even, so we won't have prime.  So the sequence goes: $7, 19, 31, 43, 55$ but $55 \ \text{mod} \ 2 = 1$, so cubes in are equally given as two examples IID.
